  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">COMMUNITIES AND JAGUAR COEXISTENCE IN WESTERN MEXICO</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">THIS PROJECT A THIRD PHASE OF THE MIJO PROJECT, WHICH HAS BEEN DEVELOPED SINCE 2017 BY FONNOR IN COLLABORATION WITH THE NATIONAL FORESTRY COMMISSION(CONAFOR), THE WORLD WIDE FUND FOR NATURE (WWF-MEXICO) AND THE SECRETARIAT OF ENVIRONMENT AND TERRITORIAL DEVELOPMENT OF THE GOVERNMENT OF THE STATE OF JALISCO (SEMADET), WITH THE SUPPORT OF USFWS.THE JAGUAR (PANTHERA ONCA) IN MEXICO IS A PRIORITY SPECIES FOR CONSERVATION AS IT IS CONSIDERED ENDANGERED , SO IT IS IMPORTANT TO CARRY OUT AND PROMOTE ACTIVITIES THAT FAVOR ITS CONSERVATION. ONE OF THE FIVE MOST IMPORTANT CORRIDORS FOR MAINTAINING CONNECTIVITY BETWEEN JAGUAR POPULATIONS IN MEXICO IS THE WESTERN CORRIDOR. IN THIS AREA THE NORTH PACIFIC AND CENTRAL PACIFIC REGIONS CONVERGE AND COINCIDE WITH THE CORE AREA OF THE NORTHWESTERN RECOVERY UNIT (NRU) IDENTIFIED IN THE JAGUAR RECOVERY PLAN OF THE U.S. FISH AND WILDLIFE SERVICE (USFWS 2018).THE MIJO HAS DEDICATED ITSELF TO DEVELOPING ACTIVITIES TO PROMOTE CONSERVATION, MONITORING AND AWARENESS OF THE JAGUAR AND ITS HABITAT IN THREE AREAS OF THE BIODIVERSITY ENDOWMENT FUND (BEF) (CONSERVATION PROGRAM BASED ON ECONOMIC INCENTIVES): SIERRA DE CACOMA, SIERRA DE ACAPONETA, AND COSTA NORTE, LOCATED WITHIN THE JAGUAR S WESTERN CORRIDOR.IN THIS THIRD PHASE, OUR GOAL IS THAT THE COMMUNITIES THAT INHABIT THE JAGUAR CORRIDOR</narrative>
  </description>
